<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyTitle>Quartz.NET HTTP Client</AssemblyTitle>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Title>Quartz.NET HTTP Client</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET HTTP client - an IScheduler that drives a scheduler in another process over the HTTP API Quartz.AspNetCore serves; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);http;httpclient;rest;remote;client</PackageTags>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.csproj makes, and read the same way.
      IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers, all of which were
      already on here (issue #3431); what it adds is the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary.Wire references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a
      native executable on Windows, Linux and macOS on every pull request, and is run. It drives a scheduler
      served by Quartz.AspNetCore over a loopback socket - metadata, scheduling, reading back, a 404 with
      problem details, pause and resume, triggering, the execution history and its statistics, the
      server-sent event stream, a listing from a host newer than the client - every body read and written
      through the wire contract's generated metadata with reflection-based System.Text.Json switched off.

      What the baseline still records, and a native publish still reports: one IL2026, a job read back over
      HTTP carrying its type as a string, in TrimAnalysisBaseline.cs beside this file. It is trimming, not
      dynamic code - the package produces no IL3050 at all - and an application keeps its job types the
      ordinary way, by referencing them from AddJob<T>() or JobBuilder.Create<T>().

      Where it stops: the claim is this assembly's. Microsoft.Extensions.Http underneath it says
      IsAotCompatible for itself. On SDK 10.0.400 the property writes [AssemblyMetadata("IsAotCompatible",
      "True")] into the assembly and nothing into the nuspec, checked by packing.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
